  "design": {
    "decision": "Decide whether to bid nil on a hand whose small cards are now all [2]s that can never win a trick when following suit; the partner decides how much to carry.",
    "opponent": "Revealed at Opening; opponents see a hand full of [2]s and must bust the nil by leading suits where the nil bidder is short or holds a stray honor.",
    "rationale": "The commons pass showed that random changes to [2]s are disastrous (-20 to -24), because they hit honors. This version touches only [3]-[6], cards that almost never win a trick when following suit, so the contract loses little while nil safety improves sharply: a [2] can never be forced to win. That makes it worth buying before any build for a team that ever bids nil, which pays well in plain Spades (a made nil is worth as much as a 10-trick contract). The intended cost is that low cards tie among themselves after the change.",
    "partners": [
      "cc-two-hold-mult",
      "uc-three-twos-x",
      "cc-nil-made-mult",
      "seed-nil-bid-mult",
      "cc-two-win-mult",
      "seed-two-win"
    ],
    "cluster": "C",
    "draftText": "Opening: Every [3] through [6] your team holds becomes a [2]",
    "hooks": "none",
    "critic": {
      "verdict": "keep",
      "concerns": "[3]-[6] rarely win anyway, so its main effect is feeding [2] payoffs, edging toward the excluded rename-for-payoffs family; the design note's 'revealed at Opening' is wrong.",
      "elegance": 3
    }
  },
